The Buddhist theory of consciousness is one of the most elaborate and sophisticated systems of psychology and philosophy developed in the ancient world. It is multifaceted, but its core principle is the understanding of consciousness not as a permanent, unitary self or soul, but as an impermanent, ever-changing process that arises in dependence on conditions.

Here are the key components of the Buddhist theory of consciousness, drawing primarily from the Abhidharma and Yogācāra (Consciousness-Only) schools:

I. The Six Consciousnesses (Viññāṇa)
The earliest and most fundamental model is based on the six sense bases, establishing that consciousness is always directional and dependent on an object and a sense organ.

Eye-consciousness (seeing)

Ear-consciousness (hearing)

Nose-consciousness (smelling)

Tongue-consciousness (tasting)

Body-consciousness (touching)

Mind-consciousness (Manovijñāna): This is the consciousness that cognizes mental objects (thoughts, ideas, memories, emotions, etc.) and also integrates the data from the five physical senses into a coherent picture. It is the "thinking" mind.

